Write-up for PA5
----------------


Implementation
--------------

The generator builds the inheritance tree and assigns preorder class tags. A
class and all its descendants occupy a contiguous tag interval, allowing case
branches to be tested in decreasing depth order. Each class layout copies its
parent's attribute and method layouts; overrides replace a dispatch slot and
new methods append slots. The same layouts drive prototype objects, class name
and object tables, dispatch tables, initializers and expression generation.

Expressions return an object pointer in $a0. Methods and initializers save $fp,
$s0 and $ra; $s0 holds self. Actual arguments are evaluated left to right,
pushed before the receiver, and removed by the callee. Lexical bindings and
intermediate objects are stored on the stack, with offsets relative to $fp;
their scopes are tracked while emitting code. Attributes live at fixed offsets
in self. Parent initializers run first, followed by explicitly initialized
attributes in source order. Basic type defaults are shared constants; other
references default to void. Arithmetic copies an Int before modifying its value.
new SELF_TYPE uses the runtime class tag to select both prototype and initializer.

Dispatch and case expressions invoke the runtime abort routines on void, and
case invokes the no-match abort if no branch applies. Assignments to attributes,
including initializer stores, call _GenGC_Assign when the generational collector
is selected. Values that must survive allocations reside on the scanned stack or
in self, and the collector flags are passed through to the runtime.

Testing
-------

example.cl exercises overriding, static and dynamic dispatch, inherited state,
SELF_TYPE, case specificity, loops, arithmetic, strings and comparisons.
tests/run.sh additionally checks nested scopes, argument order, default values,
object and value equality, all three required runtime errors, and repeated
allocation/attribute writes under -g -t. It uses the supplied reference front
end and SPIM via qemu-i386 (set RUN32='' on a host that supports native i386).
On systems where libfl requires an unresolved yylex, build with `make cgen LIB=`;
the generated AST scanner already supplies the necessary scanner code.
